1.1 Writing a simple C++ program

int main()
{
  return 0;
}

Running the Compiler in the Command Line (Windows)
- assume our main program is in a file name prog1.cc

$ prog1
$ .\prog1

Running the Compiler in the Command Line (UNIX)

$ a.out
$.\a.out

Issuing echo command:

UNIX:
$ echo $? 

Windows:
$ echo %ERRORLEVEL%

Running the GNU or Microsoft Compilers:
$ g++ -o prog1.cc
Here, $ is the system prompt. The -o prog1 is an argument to the compiler and names the file in which to put the executable file.
The command line generates an executable file named prog1 or prog1.exe, depending on the OS. 
On UNIX, executable files have no suffixl on Windows, the suffix is .exe. If -o is omitted, the compiler generates an executable named
a.out on UNIX systems and a.exe on Windows. 

C:\Users\me]Programs >c1 /EHsc prog1.cpp

is the system prompt and \Users\me\Programs is the name of the current directory (aka the current folder).
The c1 command invokes the compiler and /EHsc is the compiler option that turns on stnadard exception handling.

Compilers usually include options to generate warnings about problematic constructs.
Our preference is to use -Wall with the GNU compiler and /W4 with the Microsoft compilers

1.2 A first look at IO

* C++ language does not define any statements to do input or output (IO)
* C++ includes an extensive standard library that provides IO
* iostream library --> istream and ostream

A stream is a sequence of characters read from or written to an IO device.
The term stream is intended to suggest that the characters are generated, or consumed, sequentially over time.

Standard IO objects
* Defines 4 IO objects
* cin --> standard input
* cout --> standard output
* cerr --> standard error
* clog --> general info about the execution of the program










